    "topic_code": "1.5",
    "topic_title": "Atomic Structure and Electron Configuration",
    "stem": "In a simplified model of an atom, an electron is moved from a distance r to a distance 2r from the nucleus. The nuclear charge and the electron’s charge remain unchanged. How does the magnitude of the attractive electrostatic force on the electron change?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "It decreases to one-half of its original value.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Inverse-linear trap: This treats the force as proportional to 1/r rather than 1/r². The distance must be squared when calculating the force ratio. Fix: Square the distance-change factor before taking its reciprocal."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "It increases to four times its original value.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Direct-square trap: This treats the force as proportional to r² instead of 1/r². Increasing the separation weakens the attraction when both charges remain unchanged. Fix: Put the squared distance-change factor in the denominator of the force ratio."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "It remains unchanged.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Charge-only trap: Unchanged charges do not guarantee an unchanged force. Coulomb’s law also depends on the separation between the electron and nucleus, which doubles here. Fix: Check both the charge product and the separation before comparing forces."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "It decreases to one-fourth of its original value.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Coulomb’s law gives a force magnitude proportional to 1/r² when the charges remain constant. Doubling the distance therefore multiplies the force by 1/2² = 1/4."
     }
    ]
   },
